clark county school district reaching a  tentative agreement with the largest  teachers union and this allows for the  possibility of some of our younger  students and staff  returning to the classroom in january 8  news now reporter kristen drummond shows  us what to expect if the plan is  approved by the board of trustees next  month  the plan right now focuses on the  district's youngest students so  those who are in elementary school ccsd  says pre-k through third grade students  may  possibly return first for face-to-face  instruction  next year our babies need to be in  school  they're the ones that need the social  interaction a big step in bringing  students back to schools for in-person  learning the district and clark county  education association came to a  tentative agreement  following months of negotiations the  plan outlines certain standards and  safety precautions  that includes mandatory covet-19 testing  for educators  and providing personal protective  equipment cca's executive director calls  reopening schools a trial and error  process but  this allows for an incremental approach  being  ready with a plan is absolutely critical  and we think this is a plan that allows  flexibility  allows us to address emergent issues  that suggest  maybe we expand maybe we contract the  district will not require students to  take a covid19 test before coming back  on campus  as for the vaccine the district plans to  work with the southern nevada health  district  to make it a voluntary option for  educators the tentative agreement will  be presented at the board meeting  january 14th  trustees will also consider a potential  reopening plan on that date  until then all staff working remotely  will continue to do so  reporting in las vegas kristin drummond  8 news now
 

